<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lutsk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lutsk is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> on the Styr river, capital of Volyn Oblast. A town of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, it became the main seat of the Lithuanian prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubart">Lubart</a>, whose brick castle still stands. In 1429 it hosted the Congress of Lutsk of European rulers. It later belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> before becoming part of Ukraine.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lubart built the brick castle at <b>Lutsk</b> that still bears his name, and his rule mixed Lithuanian princes with Orthodox Ruthenian nobles and church.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-volhynia">Duchy of Volhynia</a></li><li><blockquote>Volhynia was a fertile land of towns such as Volodymyr, <b>Lutsk</b> and Berestia, trading with Poland and the West.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-volhynia">Principality of Volhynia</a></li></ul></section>
